                "kind": "annexitem",
                "text": "\\begin{annexitem}{annex:p2.hol.5}{thm:p02-many-sorted-translation}{Reduction of strict many-sorted consequence to one-sorted consequence}\n\\begin{proof}\nFirst assume the one-sorted consequence fails under an assignment \\(s\\).  Let\n\\(\\mathcal A\\) satisfy \\(\\mathsf{Ax}_{\\mathrm{sort}}(\\Lang_{\\mathrm{MS}})\\),\n\\(\\mathsf{VarGuard}_{\\mathrm{MS}}(\\Lang_{\\mathrm{MS}})\\), and every member of\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathrm{MS}}[\\Gamma]\\), but not\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathrm{MS}}(\\varphi)\\).  Put\n\\(M_{\\mathsf s}\\defeq Q_{\\mathsf s}^{\\mathcal A}\\).  Nonemptiness and pairwise-disjointness\nfollow from the first and fourth axiom families; constant typing and function\nclosure make the restrictions of the old symbols well typed.  Restrict each\nrelation to the matching product of the \\(M_{\\mathsf s}\\)'s.  Induction on terms, then\non well-sorted formulas, gives, for every matching assignment \\(s\\),\n\\[\n \\mathcal M,s\\entails\\psi\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\mathcal A,s\\entails\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathrm{MS}}(\\psi).\n\\]\nThe atomic cases use the restrictions and same-sort equality; the guarded\nquantifier clauses use exactly\n\\(M_{\\mathsf s}=Q_{\\mathsf s}^{\\mathcal A}\\).  The all-variable assignment\nguard makes the given \\(s\\) a total matching sorted assignment, with no choice\nof default values.  Thus the restricted many-sorted \\(\\mathcal M\\) satisfies\n\\(\\Gamma\\) and refutes \\(\\varphi\\).\n\nConversely, let a strict many-sorted \\(\\mathcal M,s\\) satisfy \\(\\Gamma\\) and\nrefute \\(\\varphi\\).  Take the one-sorted domain to be the tagged union\n\\(\\bigcup_{\\mathsf s}\\{\\mathsf s\\}\\times M_{\\mathsf s}\\) together with one\nfresh untyped point \\(\\star\\), interpret\n\\(Q_{\\mathsf s}\\) by its \\(\\mathsf s\\)-tagged\ncomponent, tag the assignment, and transport constants and correctly typed\nfunction values.  Send every ill-typed positive-arity function input to\n\\(\\star\\), a simultaneous choice-free extension.\nInterpret each relation by the transported correctly typed tuples and make it\nfalse elsewhere.  The four sort-axiom families hold.  The same induction gives\ntruth preservation, so the resulting one-sorted structure satisfies the\ntranslated premises and refutes the translated conclusion.  Taking\ncontrapositives in both directions proves the stated equivalence.\n\\end{proof}\n\\end{annexitem}",
                "sha256": "224807BD9656683CA51B85F011C9B354911C779D98540188150C242342C837C7"
